      "Saint Terence and Companions": 1,
      "The Maronite calendar lists Terence and his companions on October 28. We have matched this entry to the group of martyrs Terence, Africanus, Pompeius and their companions, because the names fit; the calendar itself gives no details. According to Catholic Online, they were martyred in the persecution under Emperor Decius, and tradition says they were forced into a pit filled with serpents and stinging reptiles, and those who survived were beheaded. Catholic Online gives their feast as April 10, while the Orthodox Church in America lists Africanus, Terence, Maximus, Pompeius and others of Carthage on October 28.": 1,
      "Brief story based on Catholic Online. The date follows the Maronite calendar.": 1,
      "Read about Saint Terence and his companions": 1,
      "28.10.2026": 1,
      "T": 1,
      "28 Oct": 1,
      "Saint Anastasia of Rome": 1,
      "According to the Greek life of Saint Anastasia of Rome, she lost her parents at the age of three and was raised in Rome by an abbess named Sophia. During the persecution under Emperor Decius, the city prefect Probus had her arrested. She told him that Jesus Christ was her spouse, her riches and her life, and she was tortured and beheaded. This life was written long after her death, so the details come from tradition. Her feast is October 29 in the Orthodox and Byzantine Catholic calendars. She is not in the current Roman Martyrology, and she is a different saint from Anastasia of Sirmium, whose feast is December 25 in the West.": 1,
      "Brief story based on Saints Encyclopedia. The date follows the Maronite calendar.": 1,
      "Read about Saint Anastasia of Rome": 1,
      "29.10.2026": 1,
      "A": 1,
      "29 Oct": 1,
      "Saint Macarius of Jerusalem": 1,
      "Macarius was Bishop of Jerusalem from about 312 until his death, which came before the Council of Tyre in 335. He was one of the bishops whom Saint Alexander of Alexandria warned against the teaching of Arius, and at the Council of Nicaea in 325 his name appears first among the bishops of Palestine who subscribed. The chronicler Theophanes relates that after the council the emperor Constantine ordered Macarius to search for the sites of the Resurrection and the Passion and for the True Cross; the Catholic Encyclopedia notes that excavations in Jerusalem began very soon after the council, seemingly under his direction. The Maronite calendar keeps his feast on October 30.": 1,
      "Brief story based on the Catholic Encyclopedia (New Advent). The date follows the Maronite calendar.": 1,
      "Read about Saint Macarius": 1,
      "30.10.2026": 1,
      "M": 1,
      "30 Oct": 1,
      "Saint Epimachus of Pelusium": 1,
      "The Maronite calendar lists Saint Epimachus on October 31 without details. We have matched this entry to the martyr Epimachus of Pelusium, because the name and the date fit: the Orthodox calendar keeps the Martyr Epimachus of Pelusium, who suffered in Alexandria, on October 31. The Roman Martyrology also keeps Saint Epimachus of Pelusium on October 31: as is handed down, during the persecution of Emperor Decius he saw that the prefect was forcing Christians to sacrifice to idols, tried to overthrow the altar, and was arrested, tortured and finally beheaded by the sword. According to his traditional life, Epimachus lived as an ascetic on Mount Pelusium in Egypt, working as a weaver with his companions Theodore and Callinicos. When he heard that the governor was torturing Christians, he went to El-Bakroug, destroyed some pagan idols and declared that he too was a Christian. He was tortured on the wheel and then beheaded. The Coptic Orthodox Church keeps his feast on 14 Pashons.": 1,
      "Brief story based on Wikipedia, the Orthodox calendar at Basilica.ro and the Roman Martyrology (2004). The date follows the Maronite calendar.": 1,
      "Read about Saint Epimachus": 1,
      "31.10.2026": 1,
